2fb11f98-b3ca-4b90-856b-910c231a3abf	fcfd2e31-637b-466b-b533-df70f7f1b3af	Risk Management - linkedin	The safety manager everyone thinks is "too strict" just saved his company $2.3M last month.\n\nHere's what happened: Martinez Construction in Phoenix was about to pour foundation on a 40-unit development. Their safety manager flagged what looked like a minor soil inconsistency during his daily walkthrough.\n\nThe project manager rolled his eyes. The crew groaned. "It's fine, let's keep moving."\n\nBut this "overly cautious" safety manager had been documenting everything religiously. His notes showed similar patterns on two previous sites that later had major structural issues.\n\nResult? They delayed the pour, brought in a soil engineer, and discovered unstable ground that would've caused catastrophic foundation failure 18 months later.\n\nHere's the contrarian truth: The most expensive safety managers aren't the ones who slow projects down with "excessive" documentation and protocols.\n\nThey're the ones who don't document enough.\n\nEvery skipped safety check, every undocumented concern, every "we'll deal with it later" creates exponential risk. The companies winning right now have safety managers who are borderline obsessive about capturing every detail.\n\nBecause when something goes wrong (and it will), your insurance company won't ask "Did you finish on time?" They'll ask "Show us your documentation."\n\nThe best safety managers I know treat documentation like their personal insurance policy. They've learned that 10 minutes of detailed notes today beats 10 months of legal headaches tomorrow.\n\nAt Brikly, we see this pattern constantly - contractors who invest in systematic risk documentation end up with 40% fewer insurance claims and dramatically better relationships with their carriers.\n\nWhat's your take? Are we over-documenting or under-documenting on job sites? Try our 14-day free trial at brikly.net to see how systematic tracking changes everything.	text