1bcb2ba9-9281-463a-816b-4790128c33c2	fcfd2e31-637b-466b-b533-df70f7f1b3af	Project Cost Tracking - linkedin	The most profitable contractors I know track costs backwards instead of forwards.\n\nHere's how to set up "reverse cost tracking" so you catch budget issues before they become profit killers:\n\n**Step 1: Start with your final number**\nTake your total project budget and work backwards. If it's a $180K kitchen remodel, break it into weekly burn rates. Week 8 of 12? You should have $60K left, not $45K.\n\n**Step 2: Flag the "danger zones"**\nMark the 3 phases where costs typically explode: permit delays, material delivery gaps, and finish work. Mike Torres in Denver calls these his "wallet bleeding moments" - he sets alerts at 25%, 60%, and 85% completion.\n\n**Step 3: Track daily, not weekly**\nSounds excessive, but takes 3 minutes. Log yesterday's expenses every morning with coffee. Sarah Chen from Portland says this habit alone saved her $23K last quarter because she caught a material pricing error on day 2 instead of day 14.\n\n**Step 4: Create your "oh crap" number**\nPick a threshold where you stop everything and reassess. For most contractors, it's 15% over budget. Hit that number? Full pause until you figure out the path forward.\n\n**Step 5: Build in buffer zones**\nAdd 8-12% contingency, but track it separately. When that buffer starts getting used, you know you're in trouble - even if you're still "under budget" on paper.\n\nThe contractors crushing it aren't the ones with perfect estimates. They're the ones who know where they stand every single day.\n\nBrikly actually automates most of this reverse tracking process. Worth checking out their 14-day trial at brikly.net if you want to stop babysitting spreadsheets.\n\nWhat's your biggest cost tracking blind spot?	text	"[]"	"[{\\"platform\\":\\"linkedin\\"}]"	\N	\N	draft	{}	\N	\N	2025-12-13 17:00:50.78797+00	2025-12-13 17:00:50.78797+00	\N	\N
93a36287-1c78-49bf-a2ae-9499b8454d78	fcfd2e31-637b-466b-b533-df70f7f1b3af	Project Cost Tracking - instagram	How to catch a budget blowout 3 weeks before it happens (instead of finding out when your credit card gets declined) 💳\n\nThe smartest contractors I know don't track costs daily. They track cost VELOCITY.\n\nHere's the method Mike from Denver Builds taught me:\n\n🎯 **Step 1: Set weekly burn rate alerts**\nTake your total budget, divide by project weeks. If Week 3 shows 40% spent but you're only 25% complete? Red flag.\n\n📊 **Step 2: Track the "expensive Thursdays"**\nNotice how material deliveries and subcontractor invoices always hit Thursdays? Monitor Thursday spikes - they predict your next week's reality.\n\n⚡ **Step 3: Use the 3-week rule**\nBudget issues show patterns 3 weeks before they become disasters. Labor running hot? Material costs climbing? The data whispers before it screams.\n\n🔮 **Step 4: Trust your dashboard, not your gut**\nSarah from Phoenix Renovation saved $23K last month because her real-time tracking caught a concrete overage while she could still negotiate alternatives.\n\nThe contractors winning right now? They're not just tracking money - they're tracking money MOMENTUM.\n\nYour projects deserve this level of protection. Brikly's 14-day trial lets you test drive this approach risk-free at brikly.net 🚀\n\nYou've got this! 💪\n\n🔗 Link in bio for your 14-day free trial!	text	"[\\"https://ilhzuvemiuyfuxfegtlv.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/site-assets/djpearson_construction_management_platform_with_orange_and_da_3ed78550-6c3e-4c48-beb4-58a9602b0158_0.png\\"]"	"[{\\"platform\\":\\"instagram\\"}]"	\N	\N	draft	{}	\N	\N	2025-12-13 17:00:50.911034+00	2025-12-13 17:00:50.911034+00	\N	\N
d84f8e3f-1110-4eee-8264-cb2b4e413aba	fcfd2e31-637b-466b-b533-df70f7f1b3af	Project Cost Tracking - facebook	How to catch budget problems 3 weeks BEFORE they blow up your project (instead of finding out when it's too late to fix):\n\n**Step 1: Set weekly budget check-ins, not monthly**\nEvery Friday, compare actual costs vs. planned costs for that week only. Mike from Phoenix Builders does this religiously - caught a $12K electrical overage in week 2 instead of week 8.\n\n**Step 2: Track labor hours daily, materials weekly**\nLabor costs spiral fastest. Materials you can see coming. Sarah's crew in Denver logs hours every morning - takes 2 minutes, saves thousands.\n\n**Step 3: Flag anything over 110% of budget immediately**\nNot 120%, not 150%. At 110%, you still have options. At 150%, you're in damage control mode.\n\n**Step 4: Ask "what changed?" not "who screwed up?"**\nWeather delays, change orders, supply issues - focus on the cause, not the blame. That's how you prevent it next time.\n\nThe contractors I know who never have budget surprises? They treat tracking like safety protocols - non-negotiable daily habits.\n\nIf you're tired of manual spreadsheets making this harder than it needs to be, Brikly automates the whole process. Grab their 14-day trial at brikly.net and see how much easier Friday check-ins become.\n\n💼 Transform your business today! Get your 14-day free trial: brikly.net	text	"[]"	"[{\\"platform\\":\\"facebook\\"}]"	\N	\N	draft	{}	\N	\N	2025-12-13 17:00:50.839664+00	2025-12-13 17:00:50.839664+00	\N	\N
